     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": "The single most load-bearing row is the processor column. On Bedrock and Google Cloud the cloud provider is the data processor, so Anthropic's ZDR and HIPAA arrangements do not apply at all — you use the cloud's BAA and the cloud's retention controls. [5] On the first-party API and Claude Platform on AWS, Anthropic is the processor and Anthropic's arrangements are the ones that matter. [5]"
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "Where it goes wrong."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " A team signs Anthropic's BAA and then routes traffic through Bedrock, believing they are covered twice. They are covered by AWS, under AWS's terms, and Anthropic's BAA is irrelevant to that traffic. The reverse error is assuming Bedrock inherits Anthropic's ISO 42001 certificate; certifications belong to the entity that holds them."
       }
      ]
     },
     {
      "t": "p",
      "v": [
       {
        "t": "strong",
        "v": "Example."
       },
       {
        "t": "text",
        "v": " A US health system with an existing AWS BAA and a VPC requirement runs PHI through Bedrock. A digital-health startup with no cloud footprint that wants the newest models signs Anthropic's BAA in the Console and uses the first-party API."
       }
      ]
     }
